detail.abstract:
The commercialization of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) has encouraged capital-market narratives that implicitly treat AI-native products as an extension of the traditional Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) model. This paper argues that such treatment is analytically incomplete. Conventional SaaS economics rely on the separation between the fixed cost of developing software and the low marginal cost of delivering additional access. GenAI products, by contrast, frequently require computationally intensive inference each time the customer requests an output. Every prompt may consume input tokens, output tokens, GPU cycles, memory bandwidth, routing capacity, retrieval-augmentation resources, electricity, latency-sensitive orchestration, and depreciation or cloud charges associated with specialized infrastructure.&nbsp;
<div>
 The relevant economic object is therefore not simply hosted software; it is a metered production process embedded inside a software interface. The paper introduces an Algorithmic Unit Economics (AUE) framework and formalizes Variable Inference Cost of Goods Sold (VI-COGS), Algorithmic Contribution Margin (ACM), break-even token load, and the Logarithmic Algorithmic Cost Coverage Ratio (LACCR) as audit-ready measures that connect product telemetry to financialstatement presentation. These measures are analytical diagnostics rather than IFRS subtotals or substitutes for reported gross margin. The central accounting friction is the boundary between IAS 38 and IFRS 15. Certain development-stage expenditures may, in limited and demonstrable circumstances, qualify for capitalization under IAS 38. Deployed inference, however, is generally a period cost incurred to satisfy current customer contracts under IFRS 15. When AI model training, platform hosting, and inference are aggregated into broad R&amp;D, infrastructure, or cost-of-revenue pools, a firm may report apparently resilient blended margins while negative userlevel economics accumulate underneath the segment disclosure layer.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper contributes three elements. First, it defines a measurement architecture for AI unit economics that preserves the distinction between development economics and fulfillment economics. Second, it introduces a Calibration Methodology and Traceability Protocol that maps subjective inputs, including the complexity multiplier and base compute cost, to observable evidence such as API logs, model-routing records, context-window telemetry, retrieval-augmentation events, GPU utilization, and cloud billing tiers. Third, it provides illustrative financial autopsies, an audit methodology, and an empirical research design capable of falsifying the AI margin illusion thesis. The framework does not propose a new accounting standard; it proposes an evidentiary discipline for distinguishing sustainable AI margins from subsidized compute consumption.&nbsp;&nbsp;
</div>
